# Integration Guide — User & Team Service
 
**Who this is for:** anyone on the Resolve team who needs to connect their service
to User & Team Service, or connect User & Team Service to someone else's.
**What this is not:** a rewrite of the API contract. For exact request/response
shapes, see `Contracts/User_Team_Service.md`. This doc explains *why* each
connection exists and *what to actually do* about it, in plain language.
 
---
 
## The one-paragraph mental model
 
Think of User & Team Service as a company's **HR department**. It keeps the
employee records (`users`) and the department rosters (`teams`,
`team_members`). HR doesn't work alone, though:
 
- It needs the **company directory** (Organization Service) to know which
  company (tenant) each employee belongs to — you can't hire someone into a
  company that doesn't exist.
- **Security** (Authentication) handles the actual badge/keycard printing
  (`password_hash`) — HR keeps a slot for it in the employee file, but never
  touches what's written there itself.
- **Security clearance** (RBAC) decides what each employee is allowed to do —
  that's a separate department entirely, HR just supplies the employee list
  it works from.
- Everyone else in the company (**Case Management, Workflow, Document
  Service**) occasionally needs to double-check "is this person real and
  active?" before assigning them work — they ask HR, not each other.
---
 
## Who connects to User & Team Service, and in which direction
 
| Other service | Direction | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Organization Service** (Task 2) | User & Team **depends on it** | Every user needs a real `organization_id` to belong to |
| **Authentication Service** (Task 4) | Authentication **depends on** User & Team | Authentication calls User & Team's internal endpoints to look up users and manage the one column (`password_hash`) it owns |
| **RBAC Service** (Task 5) | RBAC **depends on** User & Team | Roles and permissions attach to real user IDs — RBAC needs `users` to exist, nothing more |
| **Case Management, Workflow, Document Service** | They **may call** User & Team | To confirm a `uploaded_by`/`assigned_to` user ID is real before writing it elsewhere |
 
**The direction that matters most:** User & Team Service is mostly something
*other* services lean on, not the other way around. Its own only real upstream
dependency is Organization Service.
 
---
 
## Connection 1 — Organization Service (Task 2)
 
**Plain words:** before HR can hire someone, the company they're being hired
into has to actually exist.
 
**What to check:** does the user-creation code (`POST /api/v1/users`)
currently verify the `organizationId` it's given is real and `ACTIVE`, or
does it just trust whatever ID shows up in the request/JWT?
 
**If it doesn't check yet**, here's the shape to add — same pattern used
elsewhere in this project (a small stub function you swap for a real call
later):
 
```javascript
// src/organization/organizationClient.js
async function assertOrganizationActive(tenantId)
{
    return true; // placeholder — replace with a real call to
                 // GET /internal/v1/organizations/{id} once Organization
                 // Service is reachable, and reject if status !== "ACTIVE"
}
 
module.exports = { assertOrganizationActive };
```
 
Call it once, at the top of user/team creation — not on every read, just on
writes, same as the suspended-tenant rule described in Organization
Service's own contract (§8: writes should be rejected for a suspended org).
 
