## Connection 2 — Authentication Service (Task 4)
 
**Plain words:** this is the one genuinely two-way relationship. User & Team
owns the `users` table, but the one column that matters most for security —
`password_hash` — is written exclusively by Authentication, never by User &
Team itself. Authentication reaches *into* User & Team's table through three
specific doors, never by connecting to the database directly.
 
**The three doors Authentication uses (these should already exist, since
Authentication's login flow is built around them):**
 
| Endpoint | What it's for |
|---|---|
| `GET /internal/v1/users/lookup?organizationId=&email=` | Turn an email into a `userId`, so Authentication knows who's trying to log in |
| `POST /internal/v1/users/{id}/verify-credentials` | Authentication sends a plaintext password over the internal network; User & Team hashes and compares it, and only ever returns `true`/`false` — the hash itself never leaves this service |
| `PATCH /internal/v1/users/{id}/password-hash` | Authentication sets a new password (first-time or reset) the same way — sends plaintext, User & Team hashes it before writing |
 
**What to check, concretely:** open `src/internal/` (or wherever the
internal routes live) and confirm these three exist, and that they're gated
by service-to-service auth (`src/security/`), not open to the public
internet. If they're missing, that's the real blocker for Authentication —
nothing else in that service can work without them.
 
**The one confirmed naming conflict to fix before this goes further:**
Authentication's real JWT puts the tenant in a field called **`tenantId`**.
An earlier draft of User & Team's own contract called it `organization_id`
instead — those can't both be right. **Use `tenantId`** everywhere in this
service that reads the JWT, and double-check any code that currently expects
the other name.
 
---
 
## Connection 3 — RBAC Service (Task 5)
 
**Plain words:** RBAC doesn't ask User & Team for anything at request time —
it just needs `users` and `teams` to already exist, since its own tables
(`user_roles`, `role_permissions`) point at user IDs that live here.
 
**What to do:** nothing active. This connection is satisfied just by User &
Team Service existing and being stable — no new endpoint, no new client
code. If RBAC's team ever asks "can we get a list of all user IDs in an
org," that's just the existing `GET /api/v1/users?organizationId=` endpoint,
already built.
 
---
 
## Connection 4 — Everyone downstream (Case Management, Workflow, Document Service)
 
**Plain words:** when another service is about to write "assigned to user
X" or "uploaded by user X," it may want to double-check X is a real, active
person first — the same way Document Service double-checks a case exists
before attaching a file to it.
 
**What this needs on your side:** a generic internal lookup, if one doesn't
exist yet:
 
```text
GET /internal/v1/users/{id}
→ { "id": "...", "organizationId": "...", "fullName": "...", "status": "ACTIVE" }
```
 
This isn't in the original contract's written internal-endpoints list (which
only covers Authentication's three doors above) — it's the same kind of gap
Document Service's own contract flagged for Case Management. Worth raising
with the team at Task 0/11 sign-off rather than guessing at the exact shape
alone.
 
---
 
## Quick checklist for whoever picks this up
 
- [ ] Confirm the suspended-organization check exists (or stub it, Connection 1)
- [ ] Confirm the three Authentication-facing internal endpoints exist and are locked down to service-to-service callers only
- [ ] Fix any code still reading `organization_id` off the JWT instead of `tenantId`
- [ ] Decide, with the team, whether a generic `GET /internal/v1/users/{id}` is needed for Case Management/Workflow/Document Service, and build it if so
- [ ] Confirm RBAC needs nothing further from this service beyond `users`/`teams` existing
 




























